  "worked_solution": "Let $V$ be the volume of the ice cube. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-6\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ when $V=8\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the cube:\n\n$$\nV={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBefore we plug in the known values, we need to find the value of $x$ when $V=8\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{3} \\\\ 8 & ={x}^{3} \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug $x=2$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-6$ into the expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -6 & =3(2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sides of the cube are decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}$ at the moment when $x=2\\,\\text{cm}.$",
